## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a dynamic interval data structure that handles duplicates, supports point-sum queries, and enables k-th weight selection—all without scanning all intervals. It combines segment trees with per-node multisets to achieve range updates and efficient multi-set queries.

## Key Concepts
- Segment tree over coordinate-compressed points for efficient range queries
- Per-node multiset of (weight, id) pairs to handle duplicate intervals
- nodeSum aggregation for fast sum queries at any point
- Binary search over the weight domain combined with order statistics for k-th largest queries

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch for insertInterval/eraseInterval
class IntervalIndex {
  constructor(points) {
    this.tree = new SegmentTree(points.length - 1);
  }

  insertInterval(l, r, w, id) {
    const range = this.getCompressedRange(l, r);
    this.tree.update(range.l, range.r, (node) => {
      node.multiset.add({ weight: w, id });
      node.sum += w;
    });
  }

  eraseInterval(id, w) {
    // Find all intervals containing this id and remove one instance
    this.tree.queryById(id, (node) => {
      node.multiset.delete({ weight: w, id });
      node.sum -= w;
    });
  }

  sumWeightsCoveringPoint(p) {
    const idx = this.compress(p);
    return this.tree.queryPoint(idx, (node) => node.sum);
  }

  kthLargestWeightCoveringPoint(p, k) {
    const idx = this.compress(p);
    const weights = this.tree.getWeightsAt(idx);
    return this.findKthLargest(weights, k);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize memory for huge coordinate spaces with sparse intervals?
- Can we support range-sum queries (total weight over an interval [L, R]) without modification?
- How does this approach scale with high duplicate counts, and what alternatives exist?

Why This Is Asked
- Tests dynamic graph state and a non-trivial at-query metric.

Key Concepts
- Dynamic adjacency via hash sets, on-demand intersection, edge-id tracking, amortized performance.

Code Example
```javascript
class DynamicGraph {
  constructor(){ this.adj = new Map(); this.edges = new Map(); }
  addEdge(u,v,id){ if(!this.adj.has(u)) this.adj.set(u,new Set()); if(!this.adj.has(v)) this.adj.set(v,new Set()); this.adj.get(u).add(v); this.adj.get(v).add(u); this.edges.set(id,[u,v]); }
  removeEdge(id){ const e = this.edges.get(id); if(!e) return; const [u,v] = e; this.adj.get(u).delete(v); this.adj.get(v).delete(u); this.edges.delete(id); }
  twoStepPaths(u,v){ if(u===v) return 0; const A = this.adj.get(u) || new Set(); const B = this.adj.get(v) || new Set(); if(A.size < B.size){ let c=0; for(const x of A) if(B.has(x)) c++; return c; } else { let c=0; for(const x of B) if(A.has(x)) c++; return c; } }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to directed graphs or multi-edges? Impact on correctness and complexity?
- What if you need to maintain this metric for many (u, v) pairs offline at once—any batching strategies?

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming data often requires maintaining a running statistic under insertions and deletions. The median is robust and a common interview target for dynamic data structures.

## Key Concepts
- Two-heap approach: keep lower half in a max-heap and upper half in a min-heap.
- Duplicates supported; lazy deletion via a delay map.
- Rebalancing invariant: size difference at most 1; median derived from heap tops.

## Code Example
```javascript
class MedianDS {
  constructor(){
    this.lo = new MaxHeap(); // lower half
    this.hi = new MinHeap(); // upper half
    this.delayed = new Map();
    this.sizeLo = 0;
    this.sizeHi = 0;
  }
  _cleanTop(h) {
    const top = h.peek();
    while (top !== null && this.delayed.get(top) > 0) {
      h.pop();
      this.delayed.set(top, this.delayed.get(top) - 1);
    }
  }
  add(x){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0 || x <= this.lo.peek()) {
      this.lo.push(x); this.sizeLo++;
    } else { this.hi.push(x); this.sizeHi++; }
    this._balance();
  }
  remove(x){
    const loTop = this.lo.peek();
    if (loTop !== null && x <= loTop) {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeLo--;
      if (x === loTop) this._cleanTop(this.lo);
    } else {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeHi--;
      this._cleanTop(this.hi);
    }
    this._balance();
  }
  _balance(){
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi + 1) {
      this.hi.push(this.lo.pop()); this.sizeLo--; this.sizeHi++;
    } else if (this.sizeHi > this.sizeLo) {
      this.lo.push(this.hi.pop()); this.sizeHi--; this.sizeLo++;
    }
    this._cleanTop(this.lo); this._cleanTop(this.hi);
  }
  getMedian(){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0) return null;
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi) return this.lo.peek();
    return (this.lo.peek() + this.hi.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

